    [
        'title' => 'ARBOK GAS MASK',
        'slug' => 'arbok-gas-mask',
        'code_slug' => 'arbok-gas',
        'aliases' => [
            'arbok-gas',
            'arbok-gas-mask'
        ],
        'source_file' => 'ARBOK GAS MASK.md',
        'sector_slug' => 'sector-d',
        'sector_name' => 'Air & Climate Control',
        'readiness_slug' => 'trl-8-9',
        'readiness_label' => 'Deployment stage',
        'short_description' => 'Modern gas masks are still built around activated charcoal, a 20th-century solution facing 21st-century threats.',
        'seo_title' => 'ARBOK GAS MASK | ARBOK Technology',
        'seo_description' => 'Modern gas masks are still built around activated charcoal, a 20th-century solution facing 21st-century threats.',
        'content_html' => '<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Overview</h2><p>Modern gas masks are still built around activated charcoal, a 20th-century solution facing 21st-century threats. Chemical accidents, wildfires, aerosols, biological agents, and radioactive dust exceed the functional limits of charcoal filters in weight, lifetime, humidity tolerance, and particle capture. ARBOK introduces a next-generation gas mask filtration system based on thermally expanded graphite (TEG) — a multilayer graphene structure with an exceptionally high active surface area. The result is a lightweight, reusable, moisture-resistant filter cartridge designed for real-world CBRN conditions. This is not a cosmetic redesign but a material-level replacement of obsolete filtration logic.</p>
<p>Traditional activated-charcoal filters weigh 200–300 g, last 20–60 minutes in harsh conditions (4–8 hours in mild ones), collapse in humidity, are ineffective against viruses, spores, and sub-0.1 μm aerosols, fail above 50 °C, are single-use, and occupy 0.4–0.6 L with poor ergonomics and breathing resistance.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Applications</h2><p>Protection against toxic industrial chemicals and warfare agents; combustion products and wildfire smoke; viruses, bacteria, and fungal spores; radioactive dust and fine aerosols. Users: emergency responders, industrial workers, and military personnel — civilian and military CBRN applications.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Operating Principle</h2><p>The filter material is produced from natural graphite via thermal expansion, forming a hydrophobic, high-surface multilayer graphene structure. The material maintains filtration efficiency even when wet, unlike charcoal, due to intrinsic hydrophobicity. Particle capture efficiency reaches up to 98% for particles below 0.2 μm. Filters are regenerable on site for up to 30 cycles.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Key Parameters</h2><p>| Parameter | Value |</p>
<p>|---|---|</p>
<p>| Filter mass | 30–50 g |</p>
<p>| Volume | 0.05–0.2 L (deck-of-cards size) |</p>
<p>| Thickness | 5–10 mm |</p>
<p>| Operating temperature | Up to 450 °C |</p>
<p>| Moisture resistance | Unaffected by water exposure |</p>
<p>| Operational lifetime | Up to 80 hours |</p>
<p>| Regeneration cycles | Up to 30 |</p>
<p>| Particle capture efficiency | Up to 98% for particles below 0.2 μm |</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Architecture and Components</h2><p>TEG (thermally expanded graphite) filter element; filter housing manufactured from lightweight polymer or composite materials with impact resistance, fire resistance, electromagnetic interference suppression, and corrosion immunity — enabling operation in technologically dense and hostile environments.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Advantages</h2><p>Weight reduced from 200–300 g to 30–50 g; volume reduced from 0.4–0.6 L to 0.05–0.2 L. Lifetime up to 80 hours versus 20–60 minutes (harsh) or 4–8 hours (mild) for charcoal — field lifetime exceeded charcoal equivalents by ~20×. Operating temperature up to 450 °C versus charcoal failure above 50 °C. Performance unaffected by humidity. Effective against viruses, spores, and sub-0.2 μm aerosols. Reusable with up to 30 regeneration cycles instead of single-use disposal. Environmental: reusable filters reduce waste volume; end-of-life disposal via thermal treatment; no chemical impregnation or hazardous residues.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Integrations</h2><p>[требует уточнения из базы]</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Deployment &amp; Operation</h2><p>Filters can be replaced in seconds. Mobile production units fit inside a 10-ft container and are operable on diesel power, enabling decentralized manufacturing and crisis-zone supply, reducing logistical dependency and accelerating emergency response. On-site regeneration performed successfully in field deployments.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>TRL</h2><p>Field-tested. The system was deployed in 2023–2024 under real conditions — Chernobyl Exclusion Zone and active frontline chemical exposure zones. Observed results: filter lifetime exceeded charcoal equivalents by ~20×; on-site regeneration performed successfully; stable performance under extreme contamination and humidity. These were operational deployments, not laboratory simulations. Compliance confirmed with NIOSH 42 CFR 84 (USA), NATO AEP-54, and EN 14387 (EU). [числовой уровень TRL — требует уточнения из базы]</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Market Potential</h2><p>[требует уточнения из базы]</p></section>
