            'solution' => '<p>Modern gas masks are still built around activated charcoal, a 20th-century solution facing 21st-century threats. Chemical accidents, wildfires, aerosols, biological agents, and radioactive dust exceed the functional limits of charcoal filters in weight, lifetime, humidity tolerance, and particle capture. ARBOK introduces a next-generation gas mask filtration system based on thermally expanded graphite (TEG) — a multilayer graphene structure with an exceptionally high active surface area. The result is a lightweight, reusable, moisture-resistant filter cartridge designed for real-world CBRN conditions. This is not a cosmetic redesign but a material-level replacement of obsolete filtration logic.</p>
<p>Traditional activated-charcoal filters weigh 200–300 g, last 20–60 minutes in harsh conditions (4–8 hours in mild ones), collapse in humidity, are ineffective against viruses, spores, and sub-0.1 μm aerosols, fail above 50 °C, are single-use, and occupy 0.4–0.6 L with poor ergonomics and breathing resistance.</p>
<p>The filter material is produced from natural graphite via thermal expansion, forming a hydrophobic, high-surface multilayer graphene structure. The material maintains filtration efficiency even when wet, unlike charcoal, due to intrinsic hydrophobicity. Particle capture efficiency reaches up to 98% for particles below 0.2 μm. Filters are regenerable on site for up to 30 cycles.</p>',
