        'short_description' => 'ARBOK-AVD recovers valuable metals (lithium, cobalt, nickel, copper) from spent Li-ion and alkaline batteries via mercury amalgamation and vacuum distillation.',
        'seo_title' => 'ARBOK-AVD (Amalgam Vacuum Distillation) | ARBOK Technology',
        'seo_description' => 'ARBOK-AVD recovers valuable metals (lithium, cobalt, nickel, copper) from spent Li-ion and alkaline batteries via mercury amalgamation and vacuum…',
        'content_html' => '<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Overview</h2><p>ARBOK-AVD recovers valuable metals (lithium, cobalt, nickel, copper) from spent Li-ion and alkaline batteries via mercury amalgamation and vacuum distillation. Process: shredded battery components dissolved in mercury (forming amalgam), then vacuum-evaporated at low temperature under deep vacuum to recover mercury while metals crystallize in density-stratified layers (light metals like Li float, heavy metals like Co/Ni sink). Recovers 90–95% of target metals without harsh acids or high-temperature pyrolysis. Key advantage: compact modular units, low energy (compared to classical methods), no toxic liquid waste. Payback 6–12 months; addresses strategic supply chain vulnerability for critical materials.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Applications</h2><p>Spent Li-ion battery recycling, alkaline battery processing, e-waste metal recovery, strategic metal supply security, localized battery processing in manufacturing regions. Users: battery recyclers, EV manufacturers, battery-to-battery supply chains, critical materials traders, emerging economies building local battery recycling infrastructure.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Operating Principle</h2><p>ARBOK-AVD processes shredded battery material through a mercury-based amalgamation and recovery route. Battery cells are mechanically size-reduced and combined with liquid mercury at near-ambient temperature, dissolving the target metals into a viscous amalgam. The amalgam then passes through a vacuum distillation stage that recovers the mercury at low, controlled temperature under deep vacuum, leaving the dissolved metals behind. As mercury is drawn off, the metals precipitate in density-based layers — lithium and other light metals remain suspended longest; zinc and cadmium settle mid-column; cobalt, nickel, and copper sink toward the bottom. Recovered mercury vapor is condensed and recycled back into the amalgamation stage at high recovery efficiency (&gt;99% per cycle). The result is a set of clean, density-separated metal fractions ready for refining or direct use.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Key Parameters</h2><p>| Parameter | Value |</p>
<p>|---|---|</p>
<p>| Input (typical Li-ion battery) | 18–25% metals by weight |</p>
<p>| Metal Recovery Yield | 90–95% lithium and zinc, 85–92% cobalt/nickel/copper |</p>
<p>| Process Cycle Time | 2–4 hours per batch |</p>
<p>| Temperature (distillation) | Low, vacuum-assisted (well below pyrometallurgical thresholds) |</p>
<p>| Vacuum Level | Deep vacuum, continuously maintained |</p>
<p>| Mercury Circulation Loss | &lt;1% per cycle |</p>
<p>| Energy Consumption | 15–20 kDJ/kg amalgam (~0.004–0.006 kWh/kg battery) |</p>
<p>| Urea Product Output | 200–300 kg clean metals per 1,000 kg battery |</p>
<p>| Final Metal Purity | 95%+ for direct sale/refining |</p>
<p>| Cost per ton battery | €500–900 (vs. $1,200–2,000 for acid/thermal methods) |</p>
<p>| Compact Unit Cost (50 ton/month) | $50–100k |</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Architecture and Components</h2><p>Shredding system (hammer mill or knife shredder). Amalgamation vessel (stainless steel mixer with mercury circulation). Vertical vacuum distillation reactor with heating jacket and condenser. Vacuum pump. Mercury recovery/condensation system. Solidification/cooling trays for stratified metal layers. Material handling conveyor. Control system (temperature, pressure, safety interlocks). Compact footprint (~500 m² for 50 ton/month facility).</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Advantages</h2><p>Technical: high recovery rate (90–95%), no acid waste, compact unit fits in garage or small facility, modular scalability. Economic: 6–12 month payback (vs. 3–5 years for acid/thermal), low OPEX (mercury cycles, minimal chemical cost), scalable from pilot to 2,000 ton/month. Environmental: closed mercury cycle (&gt;99% recovery), zero liquid hazardous waste, lower CO₂ than thermal methods. Strategic: enables localized battery processing, reduces supply chain dependence on few recyclers (currently 70% concentrated in China/Korea).</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Integrations</h2><p>Battery manufacturing supply chains (major EV and battery OEMs), e-waste processors, metal refining infrastructure, EV production (battery-to-battery loops). Related: Critical Materials Recovery · E-waste Processing · Battery Supply Chain</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Deployment &amp; Operation</h2><p>Path: lab validation → pilot (10–50 ton/month) → small commercial (50–200 ton/month) → scaling</p>
<p>Conditions: controlled facility with mercury vapor containment, trained operators, ambient temp 15–30 °C</p>
<p>Maintenance: mercury system sealing/checking monthly, condenser cleaning quarterly, vessel inspection annually per hazmat regulations</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>TRL</h2><p><strong>TRL 5</strong> (Pilot operation)</p>
<p>Evidence: lab process validated, pilot units operating (reported throughput 50–200 ton/month), economics modeled, product quality confirmed</p>
<p>Remaining: 2+ years continuous operation data, cost reduction via manufacturing scale-up, industrial certifications (hazmat, metal purity), market contracts with recyclers/OEMs</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Market Potential</h2><p>Target: EV battery recyclers, battery manufacturers (battery-to-battery supply), developing markets (India, Indonesia, Africa) building local recycling. Drivers: EV production accelerating (millions of batteries/year by 2030), critical material scarcity (lithium, cobalt), strategic supply chain independence from China. Market: global spent battery recycling $10–15B by 2030; if ARBOK-AVD captures 5–10% = $500M–1.5B revenue opportunity.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Risk Factors</h2><p>Technical: mercury safety (vapor controls, operator training, spill prevention), long-term mercury recovery fidelity in continuous operation. Market: regulatory (mercury restrictions in EU/USA may tighten), competition from acid-based and thermal recycling methods with established supply contracts. Operational: operator training and safety protocols critical; mercury handling not suitable for all regions/operators.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Related Technologies</h2><p>Critical Materials Recovery · E-waste Processing · Battery Supply Chain</p></section>',
