<p>Industries and users: oil &amp; gas operators, port authorities, spill responders, environmental contractors.</p>
<p>Scale: up to 50 tons/day per unit; scalable with container sets.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Operating Principle</h2><p>Thermodynamic phase-change separation processes the oil-water-solid mixture into three streams — clean water, dry solids, and recovered hydrocarbons — without chemicals or consumables. Continuous 24/7/365 operation; works in extreme climates with thermal insulation.</p>
<p>Limitations: proof-of-concept maturity; per-unit throughput (50 t/day); recovered-fraction offtake.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Key Parameters</h2><p>Energy: &lt;1 kWh/ton. Throughput: up to 50 tons/day (scalable). Consumables: none. Form factor: 40-ft sea container (mobile or fixed; carriage-mountable). All-climate with insulation.</p>
<p>Outputs: clean water (reusable), dry sand/soil, recovered oil/petroleum.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Architecture and Components</h2><p>Phase-change separation unit; water condensation/collection; dry-solids extraction; oil recovery to storage/pipeline; control system. Containerized (40-ft), mobile or fixed, modular by container sets.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Advantages</h2><p>Technical: cleans oil from water, sand, and soil in one process; no chemicals/filters/membranes; rapid emergency setup.</p>
<p>Economic: &lt;1 kWh/ton; no consumables; recovered oil returned to value.</p>
<p>Environmental: no chemical use or hazardous emissions; solids and water reusable.</p>
<p>Strategic: mobile rapid response for spills at ports, pipelines, wells, refineries.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Integrations</h2><p>Deploys at spill sites, ports, and oilfields; pairs with ARBOK oil-recovery and sediment cleanup units; recovered oil to storage/pipeline infrastructure.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Deployment &amp; Operation</h2><p>Steps: site assessment → container deployment (mobile/fixed) → operate. Rapid emergency setup; continuous, low-energy, minimal staffing.</p></section>
