        'sector_name' => 'Organic Fertilizers',
        'readiness_slug' => 'trl-8-9',
        'readiness_label' => 'TRL 9',
        'short_description' => 'ARBOK-Bananas applies the Arbok-TRINITY acid agent (from phosphate-production waste) to the two diseases threatening the Cavendish monoculture: Fusarium wilt TR4 (a soil fungus, no fungicide, survives in soil 30+ years) and Banana Bunchy Top Virus (BBTV, no cure, spread by the banana aphid).',
        'seo_title' => 'ARBOK-Bananas | ARBOK Technology',
        'seo_description' => 'ARBOK-Bananas applies the Arbok-TRINITY acid agent (from phosphate-production waste) to the two diseases threatening the Cavendish monoculture: Fusarium wilt…',
        'content_html' => '<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Overview</h2><p>ARBOK-Bananas applies the Arbok-TRINITY acid agent (from phosphate-production waste) to the two diseases threatening the Cavendish monoculture: Fusarium wilt TR4 (a soil fungus, no fungicide, survives in soil 30+ years) and Banana Bunchy Top Virus (BBTV, no cure, spread by the banana aphid). Two tools against two enemies: a cheap acid disinfectant that inactivates TR4 spores on boots/tools/vehicles/planting material (biosecurity), and an acid mist that drives off the banana aphid within 6 hours — cutting the BBTV infection channel without killing bees. Acids neutralize in soil to water and safe compounds.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Applications</h2><p>TR4 biosecurity: disinfection barriers at plantation entrances, tools, vehicle wheels, and treatment of planting material. BBTV control: repellent treatment against the banana aphid, combined with clean suckers and removal of diseased plants.</p>
<p>Users: banana/plantain growers and export operators in Latin America, Africa, and Southeast Asia.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Operating Principle</h2><p>Two complementary uses of the same TRINITY acid solution: (1) as a spore-inactivating disinfectant breaking TR4&#x27;s main introduction route (soil on boots/tools/vehicles/saplings) — biosecurity is the only real defense; (2) as the TRINITY chitin-targeting mist that irritates and drives off the banana aphid (BBTV vector) within 6 hours, pollinator-safe. Residual acid buffers in soil to water and inert salts.</p>
<p>Limitations: TR4 is a fungus not directly &quot;cured&quot; — TRINITY breaks transmission via disinfection, not by curing infected plants; BBTV likewise managed via vector control + sanitation; banana-specific field validation pending.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Key Parameters</h2><p>Agent: Arbok-TRINITY three-acid solution from phosphate waste (&gt;6 billion t globally, 1.5 billion t USA). Aphid departure: within 6 hours; pollinator-safe; soil-neutralizing. Application modes: disinfection barriers/dips (TR4) and repellent mist (aphid/BBTV). Platform production values (Arbok-VC): deep vacuum, ambient temperature, 2–4 kWh/t feed, zero liquid effluent.</p>
<p>Note: banana-specific dose/coverage figures not quantified in source beyond the 6-hour aphid drive-off; treat as platform-derived.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Architecture and Components</h2><p>TRINITY concentrate supply (from Arbok-VC); dilution/dosing; disinfection stations (entrances, tool dips, wheel baths, planting-material treatment); repellent-mist application (sprayers/drones) for aphid control; PPE/acid handling; sanitation protocol (clean suckers, rogueing diseased plants).</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Advantages</h2><p>Technical: addresses both threats at the transmission level where chemistry fails; resistance-proof aphid drive-off; spore inactivation for biosecurity. Economic: disinfection costs &quot;pennies&quot; vs losing a whole plantation (TR4 removes land from use 30+ years); aphid repellent far cheaper than region-wide rogueing. Environmental: pollinator-safe, soil-buffered, phosphate-waste origin.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Integrations</h2><p>Application of Arbok-Repeel; fits plantation biosecurity protocols and IPM; pairs with clean planting-material programs; complements ARBOK agri platforms.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Deployment &amp; Operation</h2><p>Routine TR4 biosecurity: disinfection barriers at entrances and for tools/vehicles, planting-material treatment. BBTV: repellent aphid treatment + clean suckers + removal of diseased plants. Discipline-based, plantation/region scale. Remaining: banana-specific field validation and protocol standardization.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>TRL</h2><p><strong>TRL 5 (confirmed by Michael).</strong> Validated in relevant environment at the agent level (TRINITY mechanism proven on other pests; acid spore-disinfection well understood), but banana-specific field validation (TR4 spore inactivation efficacy, aphid drive-off on plantations) is pending.</p>
<p>TRL scale:</p>
<ul>
<li>TRL 1 — basic principles observed</li>
<li>TRL 2 — technology concept formulated</li>
<li>TRL 3 — experimental proof-of-concept</li>
<li>TRL 4 — validated in lab</li>
<li><strong>TRL 5 — validated in relevant environment ← ARBOK-Bananas</strong></li>
<li>TRL 6 — demonstrated in relevant environment</li>
<li>TRL 7 — prototype in operational environment</li>
<li>TRL 8 — system complete and qualified</li>
<li>TRL 9 — proven in operational environment</li>
</ul></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Market Potential</h2><p>Bananas/plantains support the food and income of hundreds of millions and an export trade of $8–13 billion/year, all riding on the genetically uniform Cavendish clone with no resistance to TR4 or BBTV. Affordable biosecurity + vector repellent — if validated — protects this entire monoculture where no fungicide or cure exists.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Typical Project Economics</h2><p>Disinfection of entrances/tools is low-cost versus the loss of a plantation and decades of unusable land; aphid repellent is far cheaper than region-wide removal of diseased plants. Quantitative per-hectare figures not in source — indicative; agent feedstock is negative-cost phosphate waste.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Risk Factors</h2><p>TRINITY does not cure infected plants — value depends on disciplined transmission control (biosecurity + sanitation); lapses negate it. TR4 spore-inactivation efficacy on real surfaces/soil needs validation. Aphid displacement to neighboring untreated plots. Regulatory clearance per region. Cavendish monoculture&#x27;s inherent fragility remains the underlying risk. Acid handling/PPE required.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Related Technologies</h2><p>Arbok-Repeel · ARBOK-Trinity-Rice · ARBOK-Cassava · ARBOK-Cucurbits-Triple-Strike</p></section>',
