    [
        'title' => 'ARBOK-Cadmium',
        'slug' => 'arbok-cadmium',
        'code_slug' => 'arbok-cadmium',
        'aliases' => [
            'arbok-cadmium',
            'arbok-cadmium-publishing'
        ],
        'source_file' => 'ARBOK-Cadmium_PUBLISHING.md',
        'sector_slug' => 'sector-f',
        'sector_name' => 'Organic Fertilizers',
        'readiness_slug' => 'stage-on-request',
        'readiness_label' => 'To be confirmed',
        'short_description' => 'Cadmium in phosphate rock is not an impurity a producer may ignore.',
        'seo_title' => 'ARBOK-Cadmium | ARBOK Technology',
        'seo_description' => 'Cadmium in phosphate rock is not an impurity a producer may ignore.',
        'content_html' => '<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Overview</h2><p>Cadmium in phosphate rock is not an impurity a producer may ignore. The EU caps cadmium in mineral fertiliser at 60 mg/kg P₂O₅, with a stated path to 40 and then 20. A plant has exactly one answer today: bury the cadmium together with the whole phosphogypsum stream in a stack, permanently. ARBOK-Cadmium is the same gravity column of dense brine used for phosphogypsum, applied to take the cadmium out before the stack rather than after — inside the existing wet process, without stopping the line.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Applications</h2><p>Bringing phosphoric acid and fertiliser into compliance with cadmium limits; removing cadmium ahead of the stack rather than managing it afterwards; retrofit into a running wet-process line. Users: phosphoric acid and phosphate fertiliser producers, legacy stack operators.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Operating Principle</h2><p>Milled phosphogypsum slurry is fed into a vertical column of saturated brine. Particles stratify by density: cadmium and the other heavy metals settle to the bottom of the column, radionuclides and cadmium are immobilised into a compact saline mass, and cleaned gypsum is drawn from the transition zone. The process is continuous and gravitational — no reagents, no vacuum.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Key Parameters</h2><p>| Parameter | Value |</p>
<p>|---|---|</p>
<p>| Separation driver | density, in saturated brine |</p>
<p>| Process | continuous, gravitational |</p>
<p>| Reagents | none |</p>
<p>| Vacuum | not used |</p>
<p>| Products | cleaned marketable gypsum + compact heavy-metal concentrate |</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Advantages</h2><p>Meets a regulatory limit that tightens on a published schedule, using a physical stage rather than a chemical one. Converts a permanent storage obligation — area, monitoring, compliance — into a one-time treatment that also yields saleable gypsum.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Market Potential</h2><p>The market here is set by regulation rather than by metal demand: every operating phosphoric acid plant is obliged to bring cadmium down in its product. Regions holding the bulk of world phosphate reserves with high cadmium content are the priority sites.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Related Technologies</h2><p>ARBOK-PHOSPHOGYPSUM · ARBOK-Phosphate · ARBOK-Finishing</p></section>',
        'meta' => [
            'problem' => '<p>Bringing phosphoric acid and fertiliser into compliance with cadmium limits; removing cadmium ahead of the stack rather than managing it afterwards; retrofit into a running wet-process line. Users: phosphoric acid and phosphate fertiliser producers, legacy stack operators.</p>',
            'solution' => '<p>Cadmium in phosphate rock is not an impurity a producer may ignore. The EU caps cadmium in mineral fertiliser at 60 mg/kg P₂O₅, with a stated path to 40 and then 20. A plant has exactly one answer today: bury the cadmium together with the whole phosphogypsum stream in a stack, permanently. ARBOK-Cadmium is the same gravity column of dense brine used for phosphogypsum, applied to take the cadmium out before the stack rather than after — inside the existing wet process, without stopping the line.</p>
<p>Milled phosphogypsum slurry is fed into a vertical column of saturated brine. Particles stratify by density: cadmium and the other heavy metals settle to the bottom of the column, radionuclides and cadmium are immobilised into a compact saline mass, and cleaned gypsum is drawn from the transition zone. The process is continuous and gravitational — no reagents, no vacuum.</p>',
            'market_opportunity' => '<p>The market here is set by regulation rather than by metal demand: every operating phosphoric acid plant is obliged to bring cadmium down in its product. Regions holding the bulk of world phosphate reserves with high cadmium content are the priority sites.</p>
<p>Replaces indefinite storage — area, monitoring, compliance — with a single treatment plus the sale of marketable gypsum. Payback 5–7 years.</p>',
            'use_cases' => '<p>Bringing phosphoric acid and fertiliser into compliance with cadmium limits; removing cadmium ahead of the stack rather than managing it afterwards; retrofit into a running wet-process line. Users: phosphoric acid and phosphate fertiliser producers, legacy stack operators.</p>',
