    [
        'title' => 'ARBOK-Cassava',
        'slug' => 'arbok-cassava',
        'code_slug' => 'arbok-cassava',
        'aliases' => [
            'arbok-cassava'
        ],
        'source_file' => 'ARBOK-Cassava.md',
        'sector_slug' => 'sector-f',
        'sector_name' => 'Organic Fertilizers',
        'readiness_slug' => 'trl-8-9',
        'readiness_label' => 'TRL 9',
        'short_description' => 'ARBOK-Cassava applies the Arbok-TRINITY acid agent to protect cassava — the "hunger insurance" of ~800 million–1 billion people — from its two untreatable viruses: cassava mosaic disease (CMD, 20–95 % yield loss) and cassava brown streak disease (CBSD, rots the root from inside, the worst threat).',
        'seo_title' => 'ARBOK-Cassava | ARBOK Technology',
        'seo_description' => 'ARBOK-Cassava applies the Arbok-TRINITY acid agent to protect cassava — the "hunger insurance" of ~800 million–1 billion people — from its two untreatable…',
        'content_html' => '<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Overview</h2><p>ARBOK-Cassava applies the Arbok-TRINITY acid agent to protect cassava — the &quot;hunger insurance&quot; of ~800 million–1 billion people — from its two untreatable viruses: cassava mosaic disease (CMD, 20–95 % yield loss) and cassava brown streak disease (CBSD, rots the root from inside, the worst threat). Both are spread by a single insect, the whitefly Bemisia tabaci, plus infected cuttings. TRINITY drives off the whitefly within 6 hours, pollinator-safe, cutting the main field-infection channel — affordable enough for smallholders, where expensive chemistry is not.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Applications</h2><p>Area-wide repellent spraying against whitefly during its active season; priority on cutting-propagation sites to stop the virus leaving with planting material; complements the core rule of clean certified cuttings and removal of diseased plants.</p>
<p>Users: cassava smallholders and cooperatives in Africa and SE Asia, national food-security and extension programs.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Operating Principle</h2><p>The TRINITY chitin-targeting acid mist irritates and drives off the whitefly vector (Bemisia tabaci) within 6 hours without killing bees/pollinators, breaking the CMD/CBSD transmission chain. It hits both the vector and the direct feeding damage. Residual acid buffers in soil/water to safe compounds.</p>
<p>Limitations: viruses are not cured — value is via vector control + sanitation; whitefly is insecticide-resistant (TRINITY&#x27;s physico-chemical mechanism sidesteps this); cassava-specific field validation pending.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Key Parameters</h2><p>Agent: Arbok-TRINITY acid solution derived from phosphate-industry waste streams (&gt;6 billion t globally). Whitefly departure: within 6 hours; pollinator-safe; soil-neutralizing. Application: area-wide repellent spray in whitefly season; focus on cutting-propagation plots. Platform production (Arbok-VC): proprietary deep-vacuum route at ambient temperature, low specific energy consumption, zero effluent.</p>
<p>Note: cassava-specific dose/coverage not separately quantified in source beyond the 6-hour drive-off.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Architecture and Components</h2><p>TRINITY concentrate (from Arbok-VC) → dilution/dosing → ground sprayer / drone repellent application; whitefly-season monitoring; clean-cutting certification and diseased-plant rogueing protocol; PPE/acid handling.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Advantages</h2><p>Technical: resistance-proof drive-off of a vector that defeats insecticides; pollinator-safe; breaks transmission at the vector and cuttings. Economic: cheap enough for smallholders; far below the cost of a lost field or region-wide epidemic. Environmental: pollinator-safe, soil-buffered, phosphate-waste origin.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Integrations</h2><p>Application of Arbok-Repeel; fits clean-cutting and IPM programs; complements ARBOK agri platforms; relevant alongside other TRINITY crop cards (bananas, rice, cucurbits).</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Deployment &amp; Operation</h2><p>Area-wide whitefly repellent spraying in season, prioritizing cutting-propagation sites; paired with clean certified cuttings and removal of diseased plants. Provincial/regional scale to limit vector displacement. Remaining: cassava-specific field validation and protocol standardization.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>TRL</h2><p><strong>TRL 5 (confirmed by Michael).</strong> Validated in relevant environment at the agent level (TRINITY mechanism proven on other pests), with cassava/whitefly-specific field validation pending.</p>
<p>TRL scale:</p>
<ul>
<li>TRL 1 — basic principles observed</li>
<li>TRL 2 — technology concept formulated</li>
<li>TRL 3 — experimental proof-of-concept</li>
<li>TRL 4 — validated in lab</li>
<li><strong>TRL 5 — validated in relevant environment ← ARBOK-Cassava</strong></li>
<li>TRL 6 — demonstrated in relevant environment</li>
<li>TRL 7 — prototype in operational environment</li>
<li>TRL 8 — system complete and qualified</li>
<li>TRL 9 — proven in operational environment</li>
</ul></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Market Potential</h2><p>Cassava feeds 800 million–1 billion people and is a major starch export (Thailand, Vietnam, Nigeria — the world&#x27;s largest producer). CMD alone causes an estimated $1–2 billion/year in damage; CBSD threatens East Africa&#x27;s food security and is spreading west. An affordable, resistance-proof vector repellent — if validated — protects this staple where no cure exists.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Typical Project Economics</h2><p>Area-wide whitefly repellent costs far less than a lost field or a regional epidemic, and is affordable for poor smallholders unlike conventional chemistry. Agent feedstock is negative-cost phosphate waste. Quantitative per-hectare figures not in source — indicative.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Risk Factors</h2><p>Viruses not cured — depends on disciplined vector control + clean cuttings + rogueing; lapses negate it. Cassava/whitefly field efficacy needs validation. Vector displacement to untreated plots without area-wide use. Smallholder logistics and PPE/acid handling. Regulatory clearance per region.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Related Technologies</h2><p>Arbok-Repeel · ARBOK-Bananas · ARBOK-Cucurbits-Triple-Strike · ARBOK-Trinity-Rice</p></section>',
        'meta' => [
