        'short_description' => 'ARBOK-ClosedTower is explicitly not "another tower" and not a "tower upgrade" — it is a different class of equipment in which the classic "take water from the river → heat it → cool it → return it to the river" logic stops being the foundation.',
        'seo_title' => 'ARBOK-ClosedTower (closed-loop cooling unit of a new type, replacing the classic wet cooling tower) | ARBOK Technology',
        'seo_description' => 'ARBOK-ClosedTower is explicitly not "another tower" and not a "tower upgrade" — it is a different class of equipment in which the classic "take water from…',
        'content_html' => '<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Overview</h2><p>ARBOK-ClosedTower is explicitly not &quot;another tower&quot; and not a &quot;tower upgrade&quot; — it is a different class of equipment in which the classic &quot;take water from the river → heat it → cool it → return it to the river&quot; logic stops being the foundation. Water is filled once and runs in a closed loop as a working medium: no discharge, no continuous makeup, no open contact with the atmosphere. Water is cooled to the required cold-line temperature without a waterfall and without a plume, then returns to the process.</p>
<p>The central economic argument is the delta (ΔT) — the difference between the hot line after the process and the required cold line. In a classic wet tower, a larger delta means more evaporation and more makeup water, so delta acts as a penalty. In ARBOK-ClosedTower, water losses are removed and heat recovery inside the closed cycle reaches 98% (vapor enthalpy is higher than that of liquid water; condensation returns heat into the cycle instead of throwing it into the sky), so a larger delta means more usable internal energy and better relative efficiency.</p>
<p>The second argument is biological: the classic open tower has an open basin with warm standing water, iron from corroding pipes, amoeba and biofilm, and continuous aerosol — the complete habitat and delivery system for Legionella. ARBOK-ClosedTower removes the habitat entirely rather than treating it, making the biological threat architecturally impossible.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Applications</h2><p>Cooling towers and cooling fields exist at thermal power plants, refineries, metallurgy, chemicals, pulp and paper, large compressor stations, data centers, and cold-storage terminals — different shapes, same mission: dump low-grade heat produced as a byproduct of the main process while keeping circulating water temperature inside a strict corridor.</p>
<ul>
<li>Replacement of classic wet cooling towers, fan-section fields, channels and ponds.</li>
<li>Nuclear and thermal power plants facing summer curtailment: rivers become shallow and warm, cooling water is insufficient, and heated water cannot legally be returned above temperature limits.</li>
<li>Sites with aerosol, sanitary, or Legionella regulatory exposure (nuclear plants, refineries, data centers, hospitals, hotels).</li>
<li>Sites where water is scarce, expensive, or subject to abstraction restrictions.</li>
</ul>
<p>The unit runs 24/7. If cooling fails, performance drops fast, restrictions kick in, and in the worst case the facility stops.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Operating Principle</h2><p><strong>Classic scheme (baseline being replaced):</strong> water is taken from a river, passes through condensers or heat exchangers, and leaves the process at typically 35–45 °C, reaching 60 °C on hard days. That hot water usually cannot be sent straight back to the river because it becomes a thermal shock, so it goes to a tower, becomes an artificial waterfall, meets air, part of it evaporates, and the rest cools. The water is then either discharged within limits or recirculated — with unavoidable losses and unavoidable makeup, because water is literally turned into vapor and salts force blowdown.</p>
<p><strong>ARBOK-ClosedTower:</strong> water is filled once and runs as a closed-loop working medium. No discharge, no continuous makeup, no air contact. Water is cooled to the required cold-line temperature without a waterfall and without a plume, then returns to the process for the next cycle.</p>
<p>The single change closes 5 problems at once, as stated in the source:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>CAPEX and schedule</strong> — instead of high civil works (towers, basins, fan fields), cooling becomes modular container-type equipment, scaled by adding identical modules rather than by concrete construction and months of building uncertainty.</li>
<li><strong>Sanitation and ecology</strong> — no droplet drift, no salt aerosol around the site, no living basin, no visible plume, because water is not in open contact with air as a working regime.</li>
<li><strong>Water</strong> — losses by evaporation and blowdown are removed as a technology requirement; water stays inside the loop.</li>
<li><strong>Water quality</strong> — water is cleaned of impurities, oils, salts, radiation, and dust; heavy and dissolved components are removed and delivered as a dry solid residue for further handling and disposal as solid waste.</li>
<li><strong>Control</strong> — ARBOK-Sonar monitors water parameters 24/7, feeds the control center, and on deviation either alerts operators or shifts the installation to protective mode up to shutdown, so the human factor is not the quality regulator.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Heat balance:</strong> ARBOK-TERU, as a component of the unit, recovers parasitic heat from temperature differences to cover its own needs and cuts grid electricity consumption by up to 20%. Inside the closed cycle, heat recovery reaches 98%.</p>
<p><strong>Constraints and consequences stated in the source:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Heavy and dissolved components separated from the water leave the system as a dry solid residue that still requires further utilization and disposal as solid waste.</li>
<li>Cooling is bound to the ARBOK unit&#x27;s own delta and to the set temperature of the recirculating loop, rather than to river temperature and permitted return temperature — the dependency is transferred, not removed.</li>
<li>The classic scheme&#x27;s blowdown often cannot be simply dumped; it must be treated or legally disposed of, adding cost and risk — a burden ARBOK-ClosedTower eliminates by removing blowdown.</li>
<li>Payback at high delta is described as depending on the site&#x27;s ability to accept new equipment quickly, not on the financial case.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Delta framework (why cooling duty varies):</strong> in river-based schemes, summer river temperature is often 21–23 °C, so discharged water must stay near 23–25 °C. With the process heating water to 35–45 °C, the required cooling delta is 10–22 °C. If the hot line reaches 60 °C, the delta to return to 23–25 °C is 35–37 °C. In winter the river can be 15–17 °C; if discharge must track that corridor, the same hot line produces a larger delta — 18–30 °C for a 35–45 °C hot line and 43–45 °C for 60 °C.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Key Parameters</h2><p><strong>Reference loop scale: 10,000 m³/h, 8,000 hours per year</strong></p>
<p>| Parameter | Value |</p>
<p>|---|---|</p>
<p>| Heat to remove at ΔT 10 °C | About 116 MW |</p>
<p>| Heat to remove at ΔT 16 °C | About 186 MW |</p>
<p>| Heat to remove at ΔT 36 °C | About 418 MW |</p>
<p>| Hot line after process (typical) | 35–45 °C, up to 60 °C on hard days |</p>
<p>| Summer river temperature | 21–23 °C |</p>
<p>| Required discharge temperature | Near 23–25 °C |</p>
<p>| Winter river temperature | 15–17 °C |</p>
<p>| Electricity price used | $0.12 per kWh |</p>
<p>| Makeup water price used | $1.50 per m³ |</p>
<p><strong>Classic wet tower vs ARBOK-ClosedTower</strong></p>
<p>| Parameter | Classic wet tower | ARBOK-ClosedTower |</p>
<p>|---|---|---|</p>
<p>| Electricity per m³ of circulation / processed flow | 0.060–0.070 kWh per m³ ($0.0072–$0.0084 per m³) | 0.5–1 kWh per m³ ($0.06–$0.12 per m³; $0.048–$0.096 per m³ with TERU cutting grid draw by up to 20%) |</p>
<p>| Makeup water at ΔT 16 °C | About 3.8% of circulation, about 380 m³/h | None — water in closed loop |</p>
<p>| Makeup water at ΔT 35–37 °C | About 8.7% of circulation, about 870 m³/h | None — water in closed loop |</p>
<p>| Annual water cost at ΔT 16 °C | About $4,560,000 | $0 |</p>
<p>| Annual water cost at ΔT 35–37 °C | About $10,440,000 | $0 |</p>
<p>| Annual electricity | $576,000–$672,000 | $230,000–$384,000 |</p>
<p>| Annual service and chemistry | $160,000–$480,000 | $200,000–$300,000 |</p>
<p>| Total OPEX at ΔT 16 °C | $5,296,000–$5,712,000 per year | $430,000–$684,000 per year |</p>
<p>| Total OPEX at ΔT 35–37 °C | $11,176,000–$11,592,000 per year | $430,000–$684,000 per year |</p>
<p>| Heat recovery inside cycle | — | 98% |</p>
<p>| Aerosol / drift / plume | Yes | None |</p>
<p>| Blowdown | Yes, requires treatment or legal disposal | None |</p>
<p><strong>Legionella parameters (classic open tower, from the Legionella document)</strong></p>
<p>| Parameter | Value |</p>
<p>|---|---|</p>
<p>| Open basin water temperature | 32–42 °C — Legionella&#x27;s optimal growth range |</p>
<p>| Aerosol spray rise | Up to 300 meters |</p>
<p>| Droplet drift range | 6–12 km downwind |</p>
<p>| Incubation period | 10 days |</p>
<p>| Legionella mortality, general population | 15–20% |</p>
<p>| Legionella mortality, hospitals | 28% |</p>
<p>| Philadelphia 1977 outbreak | 221 ill, 34 dead, mortality 16% |</p>
<p>| Pas-de-Calais, France 2003 (petrochemical plant cooling towers) | 86 infected, 18 dead, mortality 20%; bacteria spread 12 km from the plant |</p>
<p>| Transmission | Not person-to-person — by breathing contaminated aerosol |</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Architecture and Components</h2><ul>
<li><strong>Modular container-type cooling equipment</strong> — scaling is by adding identical modules, not by constructing a concrete tower, basin and fan field.</li>
<li><strong>Closed recirculating water loop</strong> — water filled once, no discharge, no air contact.</li>
<li><strong>Water treatment stage</strong> — removes impurities, oils, salts, radiation and dust; heavy and dissolved components are output as a dry solid residue.</li>
<li><strong>ARBOK-TERU</strong> — recovers parasitic heat from temperature differences to cover the unit&#x27;s own needs and cuts grid electricity by up to 20%.</li>
<li><strong>ARBOK-Sonar</strong> — 24/7 water parameter monitoring, transmission to the control center, alerting and automatic switch to protective mode up to shutdown on deviation.</li>
<li><strong>Circulation pumps</strong> and the connection to plant condensers/heat exchangers.</li>
</ul>
<p>Component-level specifications (module capacity, dimensions, weight, heat rejection method inside the module): [требует уточнения из базы]</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Advantages</h2><ul>
<li><strong>Water:</strong> the continuous water bill for cooling disappears entirely. At the reference scale that is about $4,560,000 per year at ΔT 16 °C and about $10,440,000 per year at ΔT 35–37 °C removed from OPEX.</li>
<li><strong>Delta becomes a resource, not a penalty.</strong> In the classic scheme a larger delta increases evaporation and makeup; in ARBOK a larger delta increases the internal energy of the process and lowers relative costs, with 98% heat recovery inside the cycle.</li>
<li><strong>OPEX does not explode with delta:</strong> ARBOK OPEX stays at $430,000–$684,000 per year across both the ΔT 16 °C and ΔT 35–37 °C cases, while classic OPEX roughly doubles.</li>
<li><strong>CAPEX advantage of $4,000,000–$6,000,000 at the start</strong>, plus a shorter schedule because there is no high civil works package.</li>
<li><strong>Legionella eliminated architecturally, not managed.</strong> The three conditions Legionella requires — standing warm water at 32–42 °C, iron/amoeba/biofilm, and aerosol delivery to human lungs — are all removed. No biocides, no Legionella testing, no basin cleaning.</li>
<li><strong>No droplet drift, no salt aerosol, no visible plume, no noise-generating waterfall, no living basin.</strong></li>
<li><strong>No blowdown</strong> and therefore no blowdown treatment, disposal cost, or associated regulatory risk.</li>
<li><strong>Independence from river conditions:</strong> the site is no longer tied to river flow, river temperature, or permitted return temperature — which is what forces summer output curtailment at nuclear plants.</li>
<li><strong>Monitoring removes the human factor</strong> as the quality regulator, via ARBOK-Sonar with automatic protection.</li>
<li><strong>Modular scaling</strong> replaces months of construction uncertainty.</li>
</ul></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Integrations</h2><ul>
<li><strong>ARBOK-Sonar</strong> — mandatory 24/7 water quality and recirculation control, with data transmission to the control center and automatic protective shutdown on deviation.</li>
<li><strong>ARBOK-TERU</strong> — parasitic heat recovery component of the unit, reducing grid electricity draw by up to 20%.</li>
<li><strong>Plant side:</strong> connects to condensers and heat exchangers in place of the classic wet tower circuit.</li>
</ul>
<p>Interfaces to plant SCADA/DCS, retrofit sequencing on existing sites: [требует уточнения из базы]</p></section>
