<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Risk Factors</h2><p><strong>Stated in the sources:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>The dry solid residue containing heavy and dissolved components (including material separated from oils, salts and radiation) requires further utilization and disposal as solid waste — the contamination is concentrated, not destroyed.</li>
<li>Deployment speed is limited by the site&#x27;s ability to accept new equipment quickly; the source frames payback at high delta as a calendar issue rather than a financial one.</li>
<li>The ARBOK CAPEX figure of $4,000,000–$6,000,000 is described as a reasonable target range for comparable duty, not as a quoted contract price.</li>
<li>The unit runs 24/7 and is critical: if cooling fails, plant performance drops fast, restrictions apply, and in the worst case the facility stops. This criticality applies to any cooling architecture, including the closed loop.</li>
<li>Higher specific electricity consumption than a classic tower: 0.5–1 kWh per m³ of processed flow versus 0.060–0.070 kWh per m³ of circulation for a wet tower. The economic case rests on the elimination of the water bill, not on lower electricity use.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Risks the technology is presented as removing</strong> (and which therefore define exposure for anyone staying on classic towers): Legionella liability, aerosol and salt drift complaints, blowdown disposal obligations, biocide and testing programs, scale and corrosion management, water abstraction restrictions, and river-temperature-driven output curtailment.</p>
<p>Commercial, certification, supply chain, and regulatory-approval risks for ARBOK-ClosedTower itself: [требует уточнения из базы]</p></section>
