        'source_file' => 'ARBOK-GOLD.md',
        'sector_slug' => 'sector-a',
        'sector_name' => 'Water Desalination & Treatment',
        'readiness_slug' => 'trl-4-5',
        'readiness_label' => 'TRL 5',
        'short_description' => 'ARBOK-GOLD recovers gold without mercury or cyanide, using non-toxic binding plus mechanical/physical separation.',
        'seo_title' => 'ARBOK-GOLD | ARBOK Technology',
        'seo_description' => 'ARBOK-GOLD recovers gold without mercury or cyanide, using non-toxic binding plus mechanical/physical separation.',
        'content_html' => '<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Overview</h2><p>ARBOK-GOLD recovers gold without mercury or cyanide, using non-toxic binding plus mechanical/physical separation. It targets sources usually considered non-extractable — seawater, low-grade ores, and even common rock — on the premise that far more gold is dissolved and dispersed than is conventionally mined. The process is low-energy, compact, and deployable in the field, and pairs naturally with desalination so that the dry salt itself becomes a gold-bearing concentrate.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Applications</h2><p>Primary pathways: (1) seawater — a binding compound captures dissolved gold during desalination, recovered by vibrational/mechanical methods; (2) gold-bearing ores — gold converted to a salt solution without dissolving the ore matrix, then physically separated; (3) any rock / low-grade ore — crushed, matrix dissolved with a natural non-toxic solution, gold precipitated with an eco-safe precipitating agent, then filtered, dried, melted, refined.</p>
<p>Industries and users: desalination plants with mineral recovery, coastal/offshore gold operations, small-scale and remote mining, geological exploration.</p>
<p>Scale: compact, mobile, decentralized units.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Operating Principle</h2><p>Gold is bound or solubilized selectively (without dissolving the bulk matrix or using mercury/cyanide), then concentrated and separated by mechanical/physical means; in the seawater route it is captured during desalination and the dry salt carries the gold and other trace metals. Eco-safe precipitation and melting finish the product.</p>
<p>Limitations: R&amp;D-stage compound/formula optimization; yields are concentration-dependent; economics best when co-produced with desalination.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Key Parameters</h2><p>Seawater yield: a small but measurable concentration of dissolved gold, varying by region; when paired with ARBOK desalination, gold along with silver, platinum and other trace metals concentrates detectably in the dry salt fraction.</p>
<p>Rock route viability threshold: economically workable even on very low-grade material that conventional mining rejects.</p>
<p>Reagents: non-toxic only (no mercury, no cyanide); eco-safe precipitation. Low energy; minimal CO₂.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Architecture and Components</h2><p>Gold-binding/solubilization stage; mechanical/vibrational separation; (seawater route) desalination + dry-salt collection; precipitation, drying, melting, refining. Compact, scalable, field-deployable.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Advantages</h2><p>Technical: extracts gold from seawater, ores, and low-grade rock; no matrix dissolution needed for ores.</p>
<p>Economic: dual-purpose desalination + gold recovery makes seawater mining viable; dry salt can be stored or declared as a gold-bearing &quot;ore&quot; asset; low OPEX vs conventional mining.</p>
<p>Environmental: no mercury or cyanide; minimal CO₂; no hazardous-material handling or disposal permits.</p>
<p>Strategic: decentralized gold for developing regions; turns desalination salt into a value asset.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Integrations</h2><p>Pairs with ARBOK desalination / zero-brine-discharge; complements brine mineral recovery; mobile units for remote/emergency use.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Deployment &amp; Operation</h2><p>Steps: source assay (seawater / ore / rock) → pathway selection → compact unit install → operation (bind → separate → precipitate → refine). Low-energy, field-capable, minimal staffing.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>TRL</h2><p>TRL 5 (confirmed by Michael). Validated in a relevant environment with field prototypes; ongoing optimization of binding-compound formulas and separation systems. Remaining: scale-up and a commercial reference.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Market Potential</h2><p>Gold mining faces mercury/cyanide bans and ESG pressure; seawater and low-grade resources are vast but conventionally non-economic. A non-toxic, desalination-paired route opens these sources and fits small-scale/remote operators and coastal mineral recovery.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Typical Project Economics</h2><p>Best economics come from co-production with desalination: water and salt carry the cost, gold and trace metals are upside; dry salt stored or processed as a gold-bearing concentrate. Standalone rock/ore routes remain viable well below the grade thresholds that make conventional mining uneconomic. Unit CAPEX is compact relative to conventional mining equipment; overall project economics scale with the ore or feed grade at the chosen site.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Risk Factors</h2><p>R&amp;D maturity — binding-compound/formula optimization; concentration-dependent yields; offtake and assay/grade verification; conservative gold sector; needs field reference at scale.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Related Technologies</h2><p>DirtyGold · ARBOK-CHLORIDE · Vacuum Osmosis</p></section>',
