        'content_html' => '<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Overview</h2><p>ARBOK-Repeel-Vineyard applies the TRINITY acid agent to two major fungal diseases of grapevines: powdery mildew (Erysiphe necator, oidium) and downy mildew (Plasmopara viticola). Both diseases reduce yield and fruit quality; conventional fungicides face resistance buildup and organic producers lack effective options. TRINITY acts both as a preventive spray and as a curative for early mildew arrest — the acid agent, at low concentration, irritates fungal spore germination without harming leaf or fruit. Applied evening/night, the acid neutralizes in hours to water and benign salts. High-value crop (wine quality sensitive to spray residue), so TRINITY&#x27;s rapid neutralization and organic-compatible profile are strategic advantages.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Applications</h2><p>Primary use cases: powdery mildew prevention and early curative on wine and table-grape vineyards; downy mildew suppression; phylloxera vector (leafhopper) repellent.</p>
<p>Geographic focus: France (Bordeaux, Burgundy, Loire Valley, Provence), Italy, Spain, Germany, California, Australia.</p>
<p>Typical vineyard: 10–1,000 ha; 2–4 seasonal sprays (summer phenological windows). Users: wine producers, vineyard cooperatives, organic certification bodies, pesticide-free growers.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Operating Principle</h2><p>TRINITY mist (~30 µm droplets) applied evening/night; the acid agent, at low concentration, irritates fungal spore germination on leaf/fruit surfaces, aborting infection. Does not kill fungus post-infection (no curative for established mildew), but prevents new infections and arrests early-stage disease. Downy mildew vectors (leafhoppers) are also repelled. Acid layer neutralizes within hours (soil buffering, leaf buffering) to water and inert salts. Residue is organic-certifiable and wine/fruit safe.</p>
<p>Limitations: fungus already deeply established in tissue (late-season) not treatable; flooded vineyards (rain immediately after spray) buffer the agent.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Key Parameters</h2><p>Agent: a proprietary multi-acid solution derived from phosphate waste. Application: fine mist; drone or airblast sprayer; evening/night (bees inactive). Timing: 4–7 day intervals during risk periods (May–August in temperate zones). Cost: $80–150/ha/season vs $150–300/ha conventional fungicides. Efficacy: &gt;90 % mildew prevention; 70–85 % early curative (if applied within 48–72 h of infection). Residue neutralization: &lt;6 hours.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Architecture and Components</h2><p>Upstream: Arbok-Evaporation/VC production of TRINITY concentrate. On-site: dilution/dosing skid; standard agricultural drone or airblast sprayer; nozzle calibration for fine mist; weather station (night-spray timing); PPE for acid handling.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Advantages</h2><p>Technical: rapid mildew arrest without residue; organic-certifiable; phylloxera vector suppression; works with existing sprayer hardware. Economic: $80–150/ha vs $150–300/ha; reduces fungicide-resistance liability; wine quality not affected (rapid neutralization). Environmental: zero bioaccumulation; organic-compliant; no water-table impact. Strategic: independence from imported fungicides; supports organic wine production; compatible with sulfur (traditional method) in tank mixes.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Integrations</h2><p>Arbok-Evaporation/VC upstream; standard vineyard drone/sprayer; compatible with Arbok-URIA (N) and Arbok-Potash (K) for nutrient programs; can be tank-mixed with sulfur or biological fungicides.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Deployment &amp; Operation</h2><p>Steps: produce TRINITY concentrate (Arbok-VC) → dilute to working strength → apply 2–4 times per season at 4–7 day intervals during high-risk periods (May–August). Evening/night application; cover upper/lower leaf and fruit. Monitoring: weekly mildew scout (visual or trap); if infection &lt;48 h old, apply curative dose. Operator: standard vineyard drone/sprayer operator + acid-handling safety training. Area-wide (cooperative vineyard blocks) recommended.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>TRL</h2><p>TRL 5 (confirmed by Michael; pilots in French vineyards). Field-validated on powdery and downy mildew in wine-grape trials; organic certification pathway explored. Remaining work: multi-year regional validation (Bordeaux, Burgundy); resistance-management studies; wine-quality long-term trials.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Market Potential</h2><p>Global wine and table-grape production: ~$200B/year; mildew losses: ~$10–15B/year. Addressable high-end wine segment (organic, biodynamic, sulfite-free): ~20–30 % of total; seeks mildew solutions without synthetic residue. Organic certification bodies actively seek alternative fungicides. Regional penetration in France/Mediterranean could capture $500M–1B/year market.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Typical Project Economics</h2><p>Vineyard: 50–500 ha; 4 seasonal sprays/year. Treatment cost: $80–150/ha/season. Mildew-damage avoidance: $200–500/ha/season (yield loss + quality discount). ROI: 2–5× (conservative scenario). CAPEX: minimal (uses existing drone/sprayer); OPEX: TRINITY feedstock + operator. Payback: &lt;1 year.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Risk Factors</h2><p>Organic certification delay (acid-agent perception, despite low concentration and rapid neutralization). Late-season mildew (post-veraison, when curative window closes) not treatable. Rainy periods buffer the agent — timing critical. Regulatory approval (EFSA, French ANSES) for wine-fruit application — 18–36 months in parallel with pilots. Grower perception: acid-based treatment still carries industrial/hazardous stigma despite the safety profile.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Related Technologies</h2><p>Arbok-Repeel · ARBOK-Repeel-Cocoa · ARBOK-Repeel-Corn</p></section>',
