        'readiness_slug' => 'trl-8-9',
        'readiness_label' => 'TRL 9',
        'short_description' => 'ARBOK-TOMATEC is a coordinated architecture of modular, containerized sub-technologies designed to achieve complete zero-waste processing of tomato industry residuals (pomace, seeds, processing wastewater) into five or more commercially valuable output streams: biofuels (via FermoFuel),…',
        'seo_title' => 'ARBOK-TOMATEC | ARBOK Technology',
        'seo_description' => 'ARBOK-TOMATEC is a coordinated architecture of modular, containerized sub-technologies designed to achieve complete zero-waste processing of tomato industry…',
        'content_html' => '<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Overview</h2><p>ARBOK-TOMATEC is a coordinated architecture of modular, containerized sub-technologies designed to achieve complete zero-waste processing of tomato industry residuals (pomace, seeds, processing wastewater) into five or more commercially valuable output streams: biofuels (via FermoFuel), biogas/biomethane, organic nitrogen-phosphorus fertilizer compounds, nitrocellulose-based biomaterials (BIOCELL), sapropel-based soil conditioners, and crystallized mineral salts. The platform operates on a resource-cascade principle: output of one stage becomes feedstock for the next. Turkey baseline: 3.5 million tons tomato paste annually → 350,000–630,000 tons pomace waste. ARBOK-TOMATEC reframes this as a multi-product feedstock, not a disposal problem. Individual modules (BUTANOIL, FermoFuel, ARBOK-Ammonia, ARBOK-Phosphate, ARBOK-SAPROPEL, ARBOK-CRYSTALLIZER) operate at TRL 9 independently; full multi-module integration is proven in operational environments. Containerized 20-foot ISO standard units; minimal staffing; remote monitoring; can co-process phosphogypsum (3 million tons/year in Turkey). Deployable as full-site integration, partial integration, or distributed hub model.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Applications</h2><p>Complete zero-waste processing of tomato paste industry residuals at production facilities (Turkey: Bursa, Çanakkale, İzmir, Manisa, Mersin clusters; global Mediterranean/Middle Eastern producers). Co-processing of phosphogypsum (fertilizer industry byproduct). Biofuel production, biogas generation, soil amendment manufacturing, nitrogen/phosphorus recovery. Secondary use: livestock feed concentrate, soil regeneration for degraded land, agricultural input sovereignty.</p>
<p>Users: tomato paste manufacturers, agricultural cooperatives, fertilizer producers, regional food-processing clusters, circular-economy integrators.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Operating Principle</h2><p>Multi-stage resource cascade: (1) Tomato pomace (skins, seeds, pulp residues) unpacked and sorted. (2) Seed stream → oil extraction (press cake for fermentation feed). (3) Cellulosic skin fraction → nitrocellulose (BIOCELL pathway). (4) Press cake + residual pulp → fermentation (BUTANOIL/FermoFuel pathway) → biofuels (C4–C6 alcohols, butyric-acid derivatives) + biogas (anaerobic co-digestion) + organic acids. (5) Process wastewater → nitrogen/phosphorus recovery (ARBOK-Ammonia/ARBOK-Phosphate modules) → mineral-based fertilizer compounds. (6) Solid residue → sapropel valorization (ARBOK-SAPROPEL) → soil conditioner. (7) Brine streams → crystallization (ARBOK-CRYSTALLIZER) → mineral salts. (8) Phosphogypsum co-processing (optional) → integrated agronomic product. Each module independently viable; combined deployment maximizes value extraction.</p>
<p>Limitations: requires co-location of modules for full cascade efficiency; per-season throughput variability (July–October tomato harvest); regional regulatory approval for biofuel/biogas outputs; scale beyond single-site requires hub-and-spoke infrastructure.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Key Parameters</h2><p>Primary feedstock: 350,000–630,000 t/year tomato pomace (Turkey baseline; 12.7M t total harvest, 3.5M t paste production). Secondary feedstocks: processing wastewater, phosphogypsum (3M t/year). Output streams: (1) Biofuel fractions (C4–C6 alcohols, butyric esters) via FermoFuel; (2) Biogas/biomethane (anaerobic fermentation); (3) N/P fertilizer compounds; (4) Nitrocellulose biomaterial (BIOCELL); (5) Sapropel soil conditioner; (6) Crystallized mineral salts. Energy profile: low net consumption; anaerobic stages produce exportable energy. Staffing: minimal on-site; remote monitoring compatible. Format: 20-foot ISO containerized modules. Throughput: 10–100+ t/day (scalable). Deployment: full-site integration, partial integration, distributed hub model. Minimum viable entry point: single module (BUTANOIL or FermoFuel), expandable to full platform. Climate: operates year-round; seasonal surge capacity (July–October harvest).</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Architecture and Components</h2><p>Modular containerized units (20-foot ISO standard):</p>
<ul>
<li>BUTANOIL (seed/pomace fermentation → biofuels)</li>
<li>FermoFuel (C4–C6 alcohol/ester production)</li>
<li>ARBOK-NITROCELLULOSE/BIOCELL (cellulosic skin processing → nitrocellulose)</li>
<li>ARBOK-Ammonia (nitrogen recovery module)</li>
<li>ARBOK-Phosphate (phosphorus recovery module)</li>
<li>ARBOK-SAPROPEL (sapropel valorization)</li>
<li>ARBOK-CRYSTALLIZER (mineral-salt recovery)</li>
<li>ARBOK-PHOSPHOGYPSUM (optional co-processing module)</li>
</ul>
<p>Interconnected via feedstock pipelines; PLC/SCADA control; automated cascade operation. No permanent buildings required; plug-and-play utility connections.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Advantages</h2><p>Technical: complete resource cascade (zero waste), each stage independently proven (TRL 9), modular &amp; scalable (entry point as low as single module), minimal staffing, remote-monitoring compatible, operates year-round with seasonal surge capacity. Economic: converts 350,000–630,000 t/year waste into five+ commercial products; biofuel + biogas + fertilizer + soil amendment + mineral salts; ROI highly favorable (waste-disposal cost elimination + multi-revenue streams); Turkey case: 3.5M t paste → 350k–630k t pomace → potential $50–200M/year value (indicative). Environmental: zero landfill, zero incineration, zero liquid discharge, net-positive energy (biogas export), soil regeneration, fertilizer self-sufficiency. Strategic: agricultural input sovereignty, circular-economy leadership, regional industrial clustering.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Integrations</h2><p>Integrates BUTANOIL + FermoFuel + ARBOK-Ammonia + ARBOK-Phosphate + ARBOK-SAPROPEL + ARBOK-CRYSTALLIZER + ARBOK-NITROCELLULOSE (BIOCELL) + optional ARBOK-PHOSPHOGYPSUM module. Compatible with existing tomato-paste manufacturing infrastructure; feedstock ready (pomace is existing waste stream); outputs compatible with agricultural markets (fertilizer, soil amendment, biofuel). Pairs with regional food-processing clusters and cooperative distribution models.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Deployment &amp; Operation</h2><p>Site selection: co-located at major tomato-paste facility or regional hub (receiving pomace from multiple producers). Installation: containerized modules positioned on-site (no permanent structures); utility connections (water, electrical, gas/biogas export). Operation: (1) incoming pomace sorted (seeds, skins, pulp); (2) feedstock distributed to respective modules (seeds→oil/fermentation, skins→nitrocellulose, pulp→fermentation); (3) cascade processing (fermentation→biogas→recovery modules→crystallization); (4) continuous operation (July–October surge; year-round baseline); (5) output collection (biofuel, biogas, fertilizer, soil amendment, mineral salts). Staffing: 2–4 on-site operators + remote monitoring. Remaining: per-region regulatory approval (biofuel standards, biogas export permits); hub-and-spoke logistics optimization; phosphogypsum co-processing validation in specific regions.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>TRL</h2><p><strong>TRL 9 (confirmed by Michael).</strong> Proven in operational environment: all component technologies (BUTANOIL, FermoFuel, ARBOK-Ammonia, ARBOK-Phosphate, ARBOK-SAPROPEL, ARBOK-CRYSTALLIZER, BIOCELL) operate independently at TRL 9 with industrial-scale deployment. Full multi-module cascade integration has been validated in operational settings, with commercial deployment models (BOOM leasing, direct purchase, JV) active.</p>
<p>TRL scale:</p>
<ul>
<li>TRL 1 — basic principles observed</li>
<li>TRL 2 — technology concept formulated</li>
<li>TRL 3 — experimental proof-of-concept</li>
<li>TRL 4 — validated in lab</li>
<li>TRL 5 — validated in relevant environment</li>
<li>TRL 6 — demonstrated in relevant environment</li>
<li>TRL 7 — prototype in operational environment</li>
<li>TRL 8 — system complete and qualified</li>
<li><strong>TRL 9 — proven in operational environment ← ARBOK-TOMATEC</strong></li>
</ul></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Market Potential</h2><p>Turkey alone: 3.5M t paste/year → 350,000–630,000 t pomace/year (currently 100% waste). Potential value per ton of pomace: $50–200 (biofuel, biogas, fertilizer, soil amendment). Annual TAM (Turkey): $17.5–126 billion (conservative–optimistic). Global Mediterranean/Middle Eastern producers: 10–15M t paste/year → 1–2M t pomace. At-stake: multi-billion-dollar waste-disposal cost + untapped resource valorization.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Typical Project Economics</h2><p>CapEx: $5–15 million (full platform, 200+ t/day); $1–3 million (partial/single-module). OPEX: minimal (waste feedstock cost ~$0), energy recovered via biogas. Revenue streams: biofuel ($800–1,500/t), biogas/biomethane ($100–300/MWh), fertilizer ($300–800/t), soil amendment ($50–200/t), mineral salts ($50–150/t). Payback: 2–5 years (full platform, Turkey baseline). Additional value: avoided landfill tipping fees ($20–50/t × 350k–630k t), avoided incineration costs, regulatory compliance (EU circular-economy targets).</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Risk Factors</h2><p>Regulatory approval (biofuel blending standards, biogas export permits) varies by region — must be obtained prior to deployment. Seasonal feedstock variability (July–October peak; lower baseline). Multi-module logistics complexity (coordinating five+ sub-technologies); operator training required. Market acceptance of outputs (biofuel, biogas certification in some regions). Hub-and-spoke model requires regional pomace aggregation infrastructure. Phosphogypsum co-processing requires dedicated validation per region. Legacy documentation spans multiple sub-technologies — full integration data not comprehensive across all geographies.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Related Technologies</h2><p>BUTANOIL (Livestock &amp;amp; Sewage Waste Recycling) · FermoFuel (Hexyl-Pentyl-Butyl Strains) · ARBOK-NITROCELLULOSE (BIOCELL) · ARBOK-SAPROPEL · ARBOK-Ammonia · ARBOK-Phosphate · ARBOK-CRYSTALLIZER</p>
<p>Слов: ~1,100</p></section>',
