<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Architecture and Components</h2><p>A stack-processing circuit built around the Arbok-Evaporation module: dissolution of phosphogypsum → recovery of 4–5 acids → crystallization of pure gypsum → evaporation and recovery of the full water volume to near-distillate quality for reuse in the wet process → fractional, separated removal of the concentrated salt/solid residue containing thorium, uranium, radium and rare earths. Associated hydrometallurgy completes separation of the individual heavies. Equipment-level component list, module sizing and train layout: [требует уточнения из базы].</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Advantages</h2><ul>
<li><strong>Resource access:</strong> turns officially worthless TENORM waste into a thorium source without opening a single new mine — purely by &quot;sterilizing&quot; existing phosphate wastes.</li>
<li><strong>Scale of the prize:</strong> the annual US PG flow alone yields 80–300 tons of thorium per year depending on grade scenario, against current US imports of 4.4 tons per year; a single Florida program covers domestic consumption dozens of times over.</li>
<li><strong>Environmental:</strong> every eliminated stack is a defused environmental bomb — the stacks carry a permanent cap of process water and grow every year.</li>
<li><strong>Co-product economics:</strong> thorium arrives as a strategic &quot;bonus&quot; to a reclamation project that also delivers acids, clean gypsum and near-distillate recycled water back into the wet process.</li>
<li><strong>Strategic:</strong> shifts thorium from toxic TENORM to domestic nuclear fuel; alternatively converts the US from a pure importer of rare earths and radioactive chemistry into a partial exporter of thorium concentrates.</li>
<li><strong>Infrastructure:</strong> the stacks are man-made deposits with known chemistry, existing roads and infrastructure — no exploration, no new mining permits.</li>
</ul></section>
