<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Risk Factors</h2><ul>
<li>Arbok-Evaporation does not do the job alone — the scheme depends on associated hydrometallurgy that is not specified in the source.</li>
<li>The 5–15 ppm thorium content in phosphogypsum is an assumption reasoned from rock grade and partition factors, not a measured value; the 60–70% recovery factor is an estimate, not a demonstrated result.</li>
<li>Demand risk is the dominant one: the entire world market is ~7,200 tons and ~$92 million, and US &quot;demand&quot; is a few tons per year — the case only becomes large if a thorium reactor program is actually launched.</li>
<li>Political dependency: the domestic scenario requires a decision in Washington to convert capacity to thorium; the source explicitly frames continued delay as the second scenario.</li>
<li>Regulatory: phosphogypsum is TENORM under EPA classification — handling, processing and residue disposal are radiologically regulated.</li>
<li>The residue concentrates radium-226 (about 80% of the ore&#x27;s radium partitions into PG) and uranium alongside the thorium, so the concentrated fraction is more radioactive than the feed.</li>
<li>No field reference for the thorium case is cited; all volumes are scenario arithmetic on open USGS and EPA data.</li>
<li>Competitive/technological: China already holds the only working thorium MSR with industrial infrastructure and continues to build the lead.</li>
</ul></section>
