        'content_html' => '<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Overview</h2><p>AZOW-Domus is the residential-scale configuration of the AZOW (Arbok Zero-Organic-Waste) vacuum phase separation platform, designed to replace connection of multi-apartment buildings to centralized municipal sewer networks. The system performs on-site treatment of all household wastewater within the building footprint, returning approximately 99% of the input flow as biologically sterile technical water suitable for in-building reuse (toilet flushing, showers, laundry, cleaning), and producing a dry organic residue (DOS) that becomes a commercial fertilizer for urban green services or agricultural use. The closed-loop architecture eliminates the need for a sewer collector, reduces municipal infrastructure load, removes building-scale methane emissions, unlocks low-carbon-water certification and significantly accelerates new-build commissioning time.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Applications</h2><p>Primary use cases:</p>
<p>• Multi-apartment residential buildings (typical reference: 5-storey 4-entrance, ~80 apartments, ~200 residents)</p>
<p>• Mixed-use buildings (residential + ground-floor retail/office)</p>
<p>• New residential developments where municipal sewage connection is expensive, time-consuming or capacity-constrained</p>
<p>• Refit of existing residential buildings with overloaded local sewer connections</p>
<p>• Buildings in water-stressed regions seeking low-carbon-water certification</p>
<p>Typical scenarios:</p>
<p>• Greenfield residential construction outside dense urban cores where city sewer extension is uneconomic</p>
<p>• Renovation projects where the city refuses additional sewer capacity</p>
<p>• Premium real-estate developments pursuing ESG / zero-discharge certification</p>
<p>• Buildings in regions with seasonal sewer overflow problems (combined sewer overflows)</p>
<p>Industries and users:</p>
<p>• Real estate developers</p>
<p>• Municipal utilities and water companies</p>
<p>• Housing cooperatives and homeowner associations</p>
<p>• Public-housing operators</p>
<p>• Urban infrastructure investment funds</p>
<p>• Smart-city programs</p>
<p>Scale:</p>
<p>• Single building module: 25–35 m³/day per AZOW-Domus unit (sized to 200-resident reference)</p>
<p>• District deployment: 20–100 buildings under a single service contract</p>
<p>• City-scale rollout: thousands of buildings in coordinated municipal program</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Operating Principle</h2><p>The system operates by deep-vacuum evaporation at ambient temperature inside a sealed vertical 4-metre chamber. Under reduced pressure, water transitions to vapor phase at room temperature without external heating. The biological load of incoming sewage — bacteria, viruses, protozoa, microplastics — is destroyed physically through vacuum collapse and ruptured by the phase-transition mechanics. Ammonia and dissolved oxygen are removed in the process; the residual water contains nothing on which pathogens can survive.</p>
<p>Process steps:</p>
<ol>
<li>Sewage intake from building drain (continuous flow or buffered)</li>
<li>Vacuum chamber phase separation (water vapor + solid concentrate)</li>
<li>Vapor condensation into a sealed condenser loop</li>
<li>Conducting condensate to the technical-water plumbing loop of the building</li>
<li>Solid residue (DOS) accumulation in sealed container</li>
<li>Weekly DOS collection for off-site use</li>
</ol>
<p>Limitations:</p>
<p>• Periodic technical service required (quarterly maintenance recommended)</p>
<p>• Backup connection to municipal sewer recommended for emergencies (optional)</p>
<p>• Drinking-water consumption point remains supplied by municipal mains (by design, not by limitation)</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Key Parameters</h2><p>Reference configuration (200-resident building, 4-entrance 5-storey):</p>
<p>|Parameter|Centralized sewer model|AZOW-Domus|</p>
<p>|---|---|---|</p>
<p>|Wastewater connection|Municipal sewer required|None|</p>
<p>|Water recovery|0% (all discharged)|99%+ (recirculated as technical water)|</p>
<p>|Energy per ton processed|50+ kWh/t (centralized treatment)|1 kWh/t|</p>
<p>|Carbon intensity of water output|&gt;10 kg CO₂/m³ (RO equivalent)|~0.034 kg CO₂/m³|</p>
<p>|Methane emission from collectors|~80 t CO₂-eq/year per building|~0|</p>
<p>|Time to operational status|6–18 months (city connection)|4–6 weeks|</p>
<p>|Indoor pipe scaling/corrosion|Standard|Effectively eliminated (sterile, mineral-free water)|</p>
<p>Typical values:</p>
<p>Throughput: 25–35 m³/day per module (sized to building load)</p>
<p>Chamber: vertical, ~4 m height</p>
<p>Footprint: 1× 20-foot container, basement room or yard pavilion</p>
<p>Energy: 1 kWh/t (~9 500 kWh/year per reference building)</p>
<p>Water quality output: COD &lt;5 mg/L, BOD₅ &lt;2 mg/L, NH₄⁺ 0 mg/L, Total bacterial load &lt;4 CFU/mL (vs EU drinking norm &lt;100 CFU/mL), zero E. coli, Coliforms, Enterococci, Pseudomonas, Staphylococcus</p>
<p>DOS output: ~9 t/year per 200-resident building, moisture &lt;15%, odorless, biologically sterile</p>
<p>DOS market value: ~150 USD/t (agricultural / municipal green services), up to 220 USD/t if pelletized to ENplus A1</p>
<p>Service life: 20 years</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Architecture and Components</h2><p>Core components:</p>
<p>• Sealed vertical vacuum chamber (~4 m height)</p>
<p>• Liquid-ring vacuum pump (inside acoustic-foamed container)</p>
<p>• Vapor condenser</p>
<p>• Condensate distribution to technical-water loop</p>
<p>• DOS accumulation vessel (sealed)</p>
<p>• Mini pellet press (~€1 000 capex, optional, weekly batch operation)</p>
<p>• Sensors (pressure, temperature, flow, water quality)</p>
<p>• PLC / SCADA control with remote monitoring</p>
<p>Auxiliary systems:</p>
<p>• Two-loop building plumbing — technical-water loop (recirculated AZOW output) + drinking-water loop (municipal supply, 10–15% of consumption: kitchen, drinking taps)</p>
<p>• Pipe colour-coding per DIN 1989 / EN 16941 (violet for non-potable)</p>
<p>• Air-gap or certified backflow preventer between loops</p>
<p>• Sampling points for periodic compliance testing</p>
<p>• Emergency municipal sewer connection (optional, for redundancy)</p>
<p>The architecture is modular and replicable. Each building receives a single AZOW-Domus unit sized to its resident load. District-level deployments may share a centralized service crew but require no central processing facility.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Advantages</h2><p>Technical:</p>
<p>• Complete phase separation, no filtration limits</p>
<p>• Stable output water with biological sterility verified by ISO methods</p>
<p>• No membranes, no chemicals, no biological activation, no consumables (except minor acid in Ammonia Trap variant)</p>
<p>• Pipe scaling and corrosion eliminated — indoor plumbing lifetime extended several-fold</p>
<p>• Operates at ambient temperature in any climate</p>
<p>Economic:</p>
<p>• Eliminates municipal connection capex (€400 k – 2.8 M per new building)</p>
<p>• Reduces commissioning time from 6–18 months to 4–6 weeks</p>
<p>• Generates revenue from DOS, technical water and carbon credits — converts cost line to revenue line</p>
<p>• 7–10 year payback; produces operating profit thereafter for 10+ years over 20-year service life</p>
<p>• Standard utility tariffs maintained for residents (no rate increase needed)</p>
<p>Environmental:</p>
<p>• Zero liquid discharge from the building</p>
<p>• Methane emission elimination (~80 t CO₂-eq/year per reference building)</p>
<p>• Carbon intensity of water output ~10× below EU Low-Carbon Water Directive threshold (0.5 kg CO₂/m³)</p>
<p>• Eliminates building&#x27;s contribution to combined sewer overflows</p>
<p>• Returns nitrogen, phosphorus, potassium and carbon to the soil cycle via DOS</p>
<p>Strategic:</p>
<p>• Building-level autonomy from municipal water-sewer infrastructure</p>
<p>• Resilience during city water outages or sewer failures</p>
<p>• Real-estate premium of 10–20% above market for certified zero-discharge buildings (active in Scandinavia, Germany, Netherlands, Switzerland)</p>
<p>• Aligns with EU Water Framework Directive, Low-Carbon Water Directive, RED III, EU Methane Regulation 2024</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Integrations</h2><p>Compatible with:</p>
<p>• New residential building construction (standard architectural integration)</p>
<p>• Existing residential building retrofit (basement / yard pavilion placement)</p>
<p>• Municipal water utility billing infrastructure</p>
<p>• District-scale DOS collection logistics (one truck route serves 20–30 buildings)</p>
<p>• Optional centralized Arbok-PAT pelletization facility for district-scale ENplus A1 production</p>
<p>• Renewable-energy supply systems (solar PV roof, geothermal heat pumps)</p>
<p>Regulatory integration:</p>
<p>• DIN 1989 / DIN EN 16941 (Germany)</p>
<p>• SIA 385 (Switzerland)</p>
<p>• BS 8595 / BS EN 16941 (UK)</p>
<p>• Arrêté 21/08/2008 (France)</p>
<p>• NSF/ANSI 350 (US states)</p>
<p>• EU Water Framework Directive</p>
<p>• EU Low-Carbon Water Directive</p>
<p>• CORSIA waste-classification for derived products</p>
<p>Digital integration:</p>
<p>• SCADA / PLC remote monitoring</p>
<p>• Sensor telemetry to municipal utility</p>
<p>• Carbon credit reporting under ETS</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Deployment &amp; Operation</h2><p>Implementation steps:</p>
<ol>
<li>Site assessment (resident load, water consumption pattern, basement / yard space)</li>
<li>Unit sizing (25–35 m³/day per building)</li>
<li>Installation of AZOW chamber, acoustic-foamed container, two-loop plumbing</li>
<li>Connection to building water-supply and sewage points</li>
<li>Commissioning, calibration, regulator approval</li>
<li>Operational handover to BOOM partner / municipal utility</li>
</ol>
<p>Operating conditions:</p>
<p>• Indoor or sheltered installation (basement or pavilion)</p>
<p>• Standard industrial electrical supply</p>
<p>• Climate-tolerant (no cold-weather restrictions at ambient temperature operation)</p>
<p>• Minimal noise — acoustic foam inside container suppresses pump noise below DIN 4109 nighttime limit (30 dB at apartment wall)</p>
<p>Operation:</p>
<p>• Fully automated, remote-monitored</p>
<p>• Quarterly maintenance visit</p>
<p>• Weekly DOS collection (1 truck serves 20–30 buildings)</p>
<p>Installation timeline:</p>
<p>• 4–6 weeks per building from delivery to operational state</p>
<p>Personnel:</p>
<p>• No on-site operator required at building level</p>
<p>• Centralized service crew (1 engineer per 30–50 buildings in district)</p>
<p>• Weekly DOS collection visit (~2 hours per building)</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>TRL</h2><p>TRL 9 (confirmed by Michael). Core AZOW process industrially validated on biogas plants in Greece and Cyprus; residential AZOW-Domus configuration engineered and deployment-ready.</p>
<p>Evidence:</p>
<p>• Continuous 24/7 operation on industrial biogas digestate (Greece, Cyprus)</p>
<p>• ISO-certified microbiological quality verification (Pankemi Lab)</p>
<p>• Validated water quality (COD &lt;5, BOD &lt;2, zero pathogens)</p>
<p>• Carbon intensity verified &lt;0.5 kg CO₂/m³ across all EU energy mixes</p>
<p>Remaining steps:</p>
<p>• First commercial reference installation in residential building (Germany recommended)</p>
<p>• 12–18 months of continuous residential operation for full TRL 9</p>
<p>• DVGW-equivalent local certification of the BOOM operator</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Market Potential</h2><p>Target market:</p>
<p>• EU residential new construction: ~2 million new apartments per year</p>
<p>• EU residential existing stock: ~220 million apartments potentially upgradable</p>
<p>• Global mid-density residential housing: tens of millions of buildings worldwide</p>
<p>Addressable share (initial):</p>
<p>• New-construction premium and mid-market segments in Germany, Scandinavia, Benelux, Switzerland, UK</p>
<p>• Real-estate developers seeking ESG / zero-discharge certification</p>
<p>• Municipalities under combined sewer overflow regulatory pressure</p>
<p>• Water-stressed urban regions (Mediterranean, North Africa, Middle East, parts of Asia)</p>
<p>Global market size:</p>
<p>• Decentralized wastewater treatment segment estimated at $30–50 billion/year by 2030</p>
<p>• Greywater / closed-loop building water systems: parallel $15–25 billion/year segment</p>
<p>Strategic positioning:</p>
<p>AZOW-Domus controls the technical platform for true building-scale closed-loop water — neither greywater systems (partial reuse) nor centralized treatment can match its combination of full sewage reuse, biological sterility and zero municipal connection requirement.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Typical Project Economics</h2><p>Reference building (200 residents, 80 apartments):</p>
<p>Throughput: 26 m³/day (9 500 m³/year)</p>
<p>Equipment service life: 20 years</p>
<p>Payback: 7–12 years depending on commercial model</p>
<p>Commercial models:</p>
<p>• <strong>BOOM</strong> (Build-Own-Operate-Maintain): partner or municipal utility owns and operates, customer pays service tariff. Reference tariff: €5.50–6.50/m³.</p>
<p>• <strong>BOT</strong> (Build-Operate-Transfer): partner operates 10–15 years, then transfers ownership to municipality.</p>
<p>• <strong>Buy/Sell</strong>: direct purchase by developer or building owner. Payback 0–2 years against avoided municipal connection capex (€400 k – 2.8 M per new building).</p>
<p>Operator revenue streams (per building per year):</p>
<p>• Service tariff (BOOM): €50–60 k</p>
<p>• DOS sale (~9 t/year × $150/t): ~$1 350</p>
<p>• Methane-avoidance ETS credits (~80 t CO₂-eq × €80/t): ~€6 400</p>
<p>• Pipe maintenance savings to building owner: indirect but significant</p>
<p>• Real-estate premium uplift to developer: 10–20% on market price (one-time)</p>
<p>Avoided costs (per building):</p>
<p>• Municipal sewer connection capex: €400 k – 2.8 M (one-time)</p>
<p>• Sewer service fees over 20 years: €500 k – 1 M</p>
<p>• Pipe corrosion / replacement: €100–200 k</p>
<p>• Methane regulatory penalty exposure: variable</p>
<p>Post-payback (years 8–20): operating profit accumulates as infrastructure rent.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Risk Factors</h2><p>• Adoption pace in residential real-estate sector (conservative by tradition)</p>
<p>• Regulatory certification timeline in each new jurisdiction</p>
<p>• Public perception of &quot;wastewater-derived technical water&quot; (mitigated by clear technical/drinking-loop separation and ISO microbiological certification)</p>
<p>• Coordination with municipal billing and utility infrastructure</p>
<p>• Operator training and BOOM partner network development</p>
<p>• Capital cycle alignment with new-construction project timelines</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Related Technologies</h2><p>ARBOK-PAT · Ammonia Trap · ARBOK MedZWD · ARBOK PURI</p>
<p><strong>Concept article:</strong> *Канализация без коллектора: концепция замкнутого водного баланса жилого дома* (Russian &amp; English LinkedIn versions, May 2026)</p></section>',
