        'readiness_slug' => 'trl-8-9',
        'readiness_label' => 'TRL 9',
        'short_description' => 'ARBOK-UNIFORMIS is a closed-loop platform for deep cleaning of heavily contaminated natural workwear and uniforms (cotton, linen, hemp, low-synthetic blends) without conventional water washing or detergents.',
        'seo_title' => 'ARBOK-UNIFORMIS | ARBOK Technology',
        'seo_description' => 'ARBOK-UNIFORMIS is a closed-loop platform for deep cleaning of heavily contaminated natural workwear and uniforms (cotton, linen, hemp, low-synthetic blends)…',
        'content_html' => '<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Overview</h2><p>ARBOK-UNIFORMIS is a closed-loop platform for deep cleaning of heavily contaminated natural workwear and uniforms (cotton, linen, hemp, low-synthetic blends) without conventional water washing or detergents. Workwear carries the highest water and chemical load of any professional laundry segment (oils, hydrocarbons, soot, biological residues). The platform removes contaminants from within the fiber structure rather than emulsifying and redistributing them, using water only to generate steam.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Applications</h2><p>Industrial workwear; service and municipal uniforms; medical/sanitary uniforms; government and defense uniforms (within civil operational standards). Material compatibility: 100 % cotton, linen, hemp, and limited-synthetic blends; no thermosensitive coatings required.</p>
<p>Users: industrial laundries, municipal services, healthcare, defense logistics.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Operating Principle</h2><p>Contaminants are extracted from within the fiber in a closed cycle; the working medium is recovered and reused (95–97 %), with water consumed only for a mandatory final steam treatment — no liquid-water washing phase. This replaces the base physics of water-based washing rather than optimizing it.</p>
<p>Limitations: working-medium chemistry/identity not specified in source; sanitation efficacy across contaminant classes and fabric types not independently validated.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Key Parameters</h2><p>Load capacity: 50–200 kg. Operating temperature: 45–55 °C. Cycle: 10–15 minutes. Residual moisture: &lt;15 %. Working-medium recovery: 95–97 %. Final steam treatment: mandatory. Water used only for steam (vs 15–25 L/kg, 40–80 L/set conventional). Garment-life extension: 30–60 %.</p>
<p>Note: figures are platform claims pending pilot validation.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Architecture and Components</h2><p>Closed-loop cleaning chamber (50–200 kg); working-medium recovery unit (95–97 %); steam-treatment stage; residue handling; control system. No detergents, alkalis, or degreasers.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Advantages</h2><p>Technical: cleans hydrophobic contaminants from within fiber; consistent sanitation; short 10–15 min cycle. Economic: up to 95 % water reduction, full elimination of chemical cost, 30–60 % longer garment life, reduced logistics/turnaround. Environmental: removes detergent/alkali load from wastewater; cuts water use to steam-only.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Integrations</h2><p>Built on the ARBOK closed-loop/vacuum cleaning platform; sibling to household ARBOK-WASH (Closed-Loop Laundry System); complements ARBOK circular-water and zero-chemistry systems.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Deployment &amp; Operation</h2><p>Steps: load workwear → closed waterless cleaning cycle (45–55 °C, 10–15 min) → mandatory steam treatment → unload (&lt;15 % residual moisture). Working medium recovered and recycled. Remaining: bench/pilot validation of cleaning efficacy and working-medium economics.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>TRL</h2><p><strong>TRL 3 (confirmed by Michael).</strong> Experimental proof-of-concept: process logic and balances defined on the ARBOK closed-loop platform, but no built industrial prototype and no validated sanitation data yet. (Supersedes the legacy &quot;scaling / industrial deployment&quot; claim.)</p>
<p>TRL scale:</p>
<ul>
<li>TRL 1 — basic principles observed</li>
<li>TRL 2 — technology concept formulated</li>
<li><strong>TRL 3 — experimental proof-of-concept ← ARBOK-UNIFORMIS</strong></li>
<li>TRL 4 — validated in lab</li>
<li>TRL 5 — validated in relevant environment</li>
<li>TRL 6 — demonstrated in relevant environment</li>
<li>TRL 7 — prototype in operational environment</li>
<li>TRL 8 — system complete and qualified</li>
<li>TRL 9 — proven in operational environment</li>
</ul></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Market Potential</h2><p>Workwear/uniforms are the largest professional textile-cleaning segment: an estimated 1.2–1.5 billion sets in continuous use, 40–60 billion wash cycles/year, 1.6–4.8 trillion litres of water and 6–10 million tonnes of chemicals annually. Current market cost $12–30 billion/year. A waterless, chemical-free route addresses this at structural scale.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Typical Project Economics</h2><p>Up to 95 % water reduction, full chemical-cost elimination, 30–60 % garment-life extension, reduced logistics. Estimated global savings $6–15 billion/year. Unit CAPEX/payback not specified — indicative until pilot.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Risk Factors</h2><p>Working-medium identity, cost, and recovery economics undefined in source. Sanitation/hygiene efficacy must be validated for medical/defense standards. Throughput and energy per kg unquantified. Possible scope overlap with ARBOK-WASH (kept separate by segment/medium — revisit if consolidation is preferred). Legacy maturity overstated.</p></section>
<section class="arbok-imported-section"><h2>Related Technologies</h2><p>ARBOK-WASH (Closed-Loop Laundry System) · ARBOK TEXTILE-WASTE · ARBOK-VC (Vacuum Cracking)</p></section>',
